    "slug": "how-to-rent-apartment-accra-ghana-2026",
    "title": "How to Rent an Apartment in Accra in 2026 — Complete Guide",
    "excerpt": "Looking for a rental in Accra? Here's everything you need to know about renting an apartment or house in Accra in 2026 — areas, prices, tips and red flags.",
    "date": "July 30, 2026",
    "readTime": "7 min read",
    "category": "Rental Guide",
    "content": "\n      <p>Finding a rental in Accra can be overwhelming — especially if you're new to the city or relocating from abroad. With high demand, rising rents and plenty of unscrupulous agents, knowing how to navigate the Accra rental market is essential. This guide covers everything you need to know.</p>\n\n      <h2>Average Rent Prices in Accra in 2026</h2>\n      <p>Rent in Accra varies hugely depending on the area, size and type of property. Here's a rough guide:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Single room (self-contained)</strong> — GHS 300–800/month in suburbs, GHS 800–2,000/month in upscale areas</li>\n        <li><strong>1-bedroom apartment</strong> — GHS 800–2,500/month in suburbs, GHS 2,500–6,000/month in upscale areas</li>\n        <li><strong>2-bedroom apartment</strong> — GHS 1,500–4,000/month in suburbs, GHS 4,000–12,000/month in upscale areas</li>\n        <li><strong>3-bedroom house</strong> — GHS 3,000–8,000/month in suburbs, GHS 8,000–25,000/month in upscale areas</li>\n        <li><strong>Shortlet/Airbnb</strong> — GHS 200–800/night depending on location and amenities</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Best Areas to Rent in Accra</h2>\n\n      <h3>East Legon</h3>\n      <p>One of Accra's most prestigious neighbourhoods. Home to embassies, multinational companies and upscale restaurants. Rent is high but the area offers excellent security, good roads and proximity to the airport. Ideal for expats and executives.</p>\n\n      <h3>Spintex Road</h3>\n      <p>A fast-growing area with a mix of affordable and mid-range apartments. Good road network, close to supermarkets and shopping centres. Popular with young professionals and families.</p>\n\n      <h3>Adenta & Madina</h3>\n      <p>More affordable areas in the Greater Accra region. Good for families looking for larger spaces at lower prices. Commute to central Accra can be long during peak hours.</p>\n\n      <h3>Tema</h3>\n      <p>Ghana's industrial city just east of Accra. More affordable than central Accra with a planned layout and good infrastructure. Popular with port workers and industrial employees.</p>\n\n      <h3>Cantonments & Airport Residential</h3>\n      <p>Exclusive diplomatic and residential areas near Kotoka International Airport. Very secure, well-maintained but extremely expensive. Mostly occupied by diplomats, NGO workers and senior executives.</p>\n\n      <h3>Kasoa</h3>\n      <p>Fast-growing satellite town on the western outskirts of Accra. Very affordable rents but heavy traffic on the main road. Good option for those working from home or with flexible schedules.</p>\n\n      <h3>Dansoman & Abeka</h3>\n      <p>Popular working-class neighbourhoods with affordable rents. Close to central Accra with good transport links. Great option for young workers and students.</p>\n\n      <h2>How Rent Payment Works in Ghana</h2>\n      <p>This is something many people are shocked by when renting in Ghana. Unlike most countries where you pay monthly, Ghanaian landlords typically require:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>2 years advance</strong> — most common requirement, especially outside upscale areas</li>\n        <li><strong>1 year advance</strong> — common in mid-range areas</li>\n        <li><strong>6 months advance</strong> — rare but available in some newer developments</li>\n        <li><strong>Monthly payment</strong> — available in some serviced apartments and shortlets</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Always budget for advance rent payments when planning your move to Accra.</p>\n\n      <h2>Red Flags to Watch Out For</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Agents asking for fees before viewing</strong> — legitimate agents show you the property first</li>\n        <li><strong>Prices too good to be true</strong> — a 2-bedroom in East Legon for GHS 500/month is a scam</li>\n        <li><strong>No tenancy agreement</strong> — always insist on a written agreement</li>\n        <li><strong>Landlord overseas</strong> — be very careful of landlords claiming to be abroad who ask you to pay before meeting</li>\n        <li><strong>Cash only payments</strong> — insist on bank transfer or mobile money with receipts</li>\n        <li><strong>Pressure to decide immediately</strong> — legitimate landlords give you time to decide</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What to Check Before Signing</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Water supply</strong> — is there reliable Ghana Water Company supply or a borehole?</li>\n        <li><strong>Electricity</strong> — check for prepaid meter and confirm it's working</li>\n        <li><strong>Security</strong> — is there a gate, security guard or neighbourhood watch?</li>\n        <li><strong>Internet</strong> — fibre availability is important if you work from home</li>\n        <li><strong>Parking</strong> — is there dedicated parking for your vehicle?</li>\n        <li><strong>Neighbours</strong> — visit at different times of day to check noise levels</li>\n        <li><strong>Condition</strong> — check for damp, mold, broken fixtures and roof condition</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Documents You Need to Rent in Accra</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Valid Ghana Card or passport</li>\n        <li>Proof of employment or business (pay slips, business registration)</li>\n        <li>References from previous landlord if available</li>\n        <li>Bank statement showing ability to pay advance rent</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Using a Real Estate Agent in Accra</h2>\n      <p>Most rentals in Accra go through agents. Agents typically charge one month's rent as commission. Always verify the agent is legitimate — ask for their Ghana Real Estate Developers Association (GREDA) membership or business registration.</p>\n\n      <h2>Find Rentals on Makola Digital</h2>\n      <p>Browse apartments, houses and rooms for rent across Accra and Ghana on Makola Digital. Connect directly with landlords and agents — no middleman fees, no hassle. List your property for free or find your next home today.</p>\n    "
