    "content": "\n      <p>Chinese cars have taken Ghana by storm. Brands like Chery, BYD, GAC, BAIC, Haval and Dongfeng are now everywhere on Accra's roads — and for good reason. They offer modern features, decent reliability and significantly lower prices than Japanese or European alternatives.</p>\n      <p>But buying a Chinese car in Ghana requires careful research. This guide covers everything you need to know.</p>\n\n      <h2>Why Are Chinese Cars So Popular in Ghana?</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Price</strong> — a brand new Chinese SUV can cost GHS 120,000–200,000 compared to GHS 300,000+ for a Toyota equivalent</li>\n        <li><strong>Features</strong> — Chinese cars come loaded with touchscreens, reverse cameras, sunroofs and lane assist at entry-level prices</li>\n        <li><strong>Fuel efficiency</strong> — many newer models are hybrid or fully electric</li>\n        <li><strong>Availability</strong> — dealers are now in Accra, Kumasi, Takoradi and Tamale</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Top Chinese Car Brands Available in Ghana</h2>\n      \n      <h3>1. Chery</h3>\n      <p>One of the most established Chinese brands in Ghana. Popular models include the Tiggo 4 Pro, Tiggo 7 Pro and Tiggo 8. Known for decent build quality and good after-sales support.</p>\n      \n      <h3>2. GAC Motor</h3>\n      <p>GAC has a strong presence in Ghana with models like the GS4, GS8 and the Emkoo. GAC vehicles are known for comfortable interiors and modern safety features.</p>\n      \n      <h3>3. Haval (Great Wall Motors)</h3>\n      <p>Haval SUVs like the H6 and Jolion are popular in Ghana. Great Wall Motors is one of China's largest automakers and Haval offers strong build quality for the price.</p>\n      \n      <h3>4. BYD</h3>\n      <p>BYD is China's leading electric vehicle brand and is rapidly expanding in Ghana. If you want an EV or hybrid, BYD's Atto 3 and Seal models are worth considering.</p>\n      \n      <h3>5. BAIC</h3>\n      <p>BAIC offers affordable entry-level vehicles popular with ride-hailing drivers and small businesses in Ghana.</p>\n\n      <h2>Things to Check Before Buying a Chinese Car in Ghana</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Spare parts availability</strong> — this is the most critical factor. Ask the dealer if spare parts are locally available and what the wait time is for major components</li>\n        <li><strong>Warranty</strong> — most new Chinese cars come with a 3-year or 100,000km warranty. Confirm this in writing</li>\n        <li><strong>Service centres</strong> — ask how many authorised service centres exist in Ghana and their locations</li>\n        <li><strong>Resale value</strong> — Chinese cars depreciate faster than Japanese cars. Factor this into your budget</li>\n        <li><strong>Fuel type</strong> — petrol, diesel, hybrid or electric? Make sure fuel is readily available for your model</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>New vs Second Hand Chinese Cars in Ghana</h2>\n      <p><strong>New Chinese cars</strong> come with full warranty and dealer support but cost more upfront. Most dealers offer financing options.</p>\n      <p><strong>Second hand Chinese cars</strong> are significantly cheaper but spare parts can be harder to find and service history is often unavailable. If buying second hand, always take the car to an independent mechanic before paying.</p>\n\n      <h2>Where to Buy Chinese Cars in Ghana</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Authorised dealerships</strong> — safest option for new cars. Most are located in Accra's Airport City, East Legon and Tema</li>\n        <li><strong>Online marketplaces</strong> — <strong>Makola Digital</strong> lists new and used Chinese cars from verified sellers across Ghana. You can compare prices, view photos and contact sellers directly via WhatsApp</li>\n        <li><strong>Car lots</strong> — second hand Chinese cars are available at car lots along the Spintex Road and Tema Motorway areas</li>\n        <li><strong>Direct import</strong> — some buyers import directly from China via shipping agents. This can save money but requires knowledge of import duties and clearing processes</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Import Duties on Chinese Cars in Ghana</h2>\n      <p>If you're importing a Chinese car yourself, you'll need to factor in Ghana Revenue Authority import duties which can add 30–60% to the cost of the vehicle depending on engine size and age. Always get a quote from a licensed clearing agent before committing.</p>\n\n      <h2>Best Chinese Cars to Buy in Ghana Right Now</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Chery Tiggo 4 Pro</strong> — best value compact SUV, GHS 130,000–160,000</li>\n        <li><strong>GAC GS4</strong> — comfortable family SUV, GHS 150,000–185,000</li>\n        <li><strong>Haval Jolion</strong> — stylish and feature-rich, GHS 145,000–175,000</li>\n        <li><strong>BYD Atto 3</strong> — best electric option, GHS 200,000–250,000</li>\n        <li><strong>BAIC X55</strong> — budget-friendly entry level, GHS 95,000–120,000</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Find Chinese Cars on Makola Digital</h2>\n      <p>Browse new and used Chinese cars listed by verified sellers across Ghana on Makola Digital. Compare prices, view photos and contact sellers directly via WhatsApp — all for free.</p>\n    "
