    "slug": "how-to-spot-fake-sellers-online-ghana",
    "title": "How to Spot Fake Sellers and Avoid Scams When Shopping Online in Ghana",
    "excerpt": "Online shopping scams are common in Ghana. Learn how to identify fake sellers and protect yourself when buying online.",
    "date": "July 5, 2026",
    "readTime": "6 min read",
    "category": "Safety Tips",
    "content": "\n      <p>Online shopping in Ghana has grown rapidly but so have scams. Whether you're buying a phone, furniture or fashion, knowing how to spot fake sellers can save you from losing money. Here's everything you need to know to shop safely online in Ghana.</p>\n\n      <h2>Common Online Shopping Scams in Ghana</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Advance payment scams</strong> — seller asks for full payment upfront then disappears</li>\n        <li><strong>Fake product photos</strong> — photos are stolen from other listings or websites</li>\n        <li><strong>Wrong item delivered</strong> — you pay for an iPhone and receive a cheap Android</li>\n        <li><strong>Overpriced items</strong> — seller inflates prices far above market value</li>\n        <li><strong>Non-existent items</strong> — the product doesn't exist at all</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Red Flags to Watch Out For</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Price too good to be true</strong> — if a brand new iPhone 14 is listed for GHS 500, it's a scam</li>\n        <li><strong>No profile photo or history</strong> — brand new accounts with no listing history</li>\n        <li><strong>Refuses to meet in person</strong> — legitimate sellers are happy to meet</li>\n        <li><strong>Asks for full payment before meeting</strong> — never pay before you see the item</li>\n        <li><strong>Poor quality photos</strong> — blurry, stock images or photos with watermarks from other sites</li>\n        <li><strong>Pressure to buy quickly</strong> — \"offer expires in 1 hour\" is a manipulation tactic</li>\n        <li><strong>Asks you to communicate off-platform</strong> — wants to move to a different app immediately</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>How to Verify a Seller is Legitimate</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Check their listing history — how many listings do they have?</li>\n        <li>Look at how long they've been a member</li>\n        <li>Ask for a video call to see the item live</li>\n        <li>Request additional photos from different angles</li>\n        <li>Google the seller's phone number to check for scam reports</li>\n        <li>Read reviews if available</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Safe Ways to Pay in Ghana</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Pay on delivery</strong> — best option, inspect before paying</li>\n        <li><strong>Meet in a public place</strong> — busy area like a mall or bank</li>\n        <li><strong>Mobile Money</strong> — only send after receiving the item</li>\n        <li><strong>Never wire transfer</strong> — impossible to reverse if scammed</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>What to Do If You've Been Scammed</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Report to the platform immediately</li>\n        <li>Report to Ghana Police Service Cybercrime Unit</li>\n        <li>Contact your mobile money provider to attempt a reversal</li>\n        <li>Share the scammer's details to warn others</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Why Makola Digital is Safe</h2>\n      <p>Makola Digital verifies sellers and allows buyers to chat directly with sellers via WhatsApp before purchasing. Our team reviews all listings and removes suspicious ones. We also encourage buyers to always meet sellers in person for high-value items.</p>\n      <p>If you encounter a suspicious listing on Makola Digital, contact us immediately at hello@makoladigital.online.</p>\n    "
